WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.880
What do you get when you give two&nbsp;
morons a baseball bat and a frog?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:09.120
One of the most successful -- and&nbsp;
controversial -- animated series of all time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.080
This is the untold truth of Beavis and Butt-Head.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:17.680
As a high schooler, Mike Judge loved to&nbsp;
doodle in his notebooks during class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:23.280
but he eventually pursued a career in engineering.&nbsp;
When he happened to attend an animation festival,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:28.080
however, he rediscovered his longtime love&nbsp;
of cartooning. He revealed to the AV Club:

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.040
"I'd always wanted to try animation. I thought of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.640
animation as something I wanted to&nbsp;
try if I was ever rich or retired."

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:41.520
Judge spent $200 on a Bolex camera, acquired&nbsp;
a lightbox, some ink and paper, and drew a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:47.280
cartoon about a nerdy office worker named&nbsp;
Milton. Judge sold his cartoon, Office Space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:54.240
to the now-nonexistent Comedy Channel for $2,000,&nbsp;
and it went on to inspire the 1999 comedy of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:59.120
same name. That was when Judge realized that&nbsp;
making cartoons could be a full-time job.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.160
"Then we'll be able to get&nbsp;
money and chicks and stuff."

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.520
"Yeah. Yeah! We are there."

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.680
Judge canceled his plan to become a math&nbsp;
teacher and created another animated short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:17.760
called Frog Baseball. The short starred&nbsp;
two deviant youths — Beavis and Butt-Head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:24.320
both voiced by Judge — violently abusing a&nbsp;
small amphibian . MTV's animation showcase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.320
Liquid Television bought the short,&nbsp;
and network executives liked it so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.720
much that they signed Judge to produce&nbsp;
65 episodes of Beavis and Butt-Head.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:39.680
Not only are Beavis and Butt-Head among the most&nbsp;
uniquely unintelligent characters in TV history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:44.400
they've got two of the most original&nbsp;
names seen on the small screen. Creator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:49.360
Mike Judge got those monikers from real-life&nbsp;
inspirations, telling Wild Cartoon Kingdom:

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.360
"When I was in college, a 12-year-old kid next&nbsp;
door called himself Iron Butt. [...] His friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:59.120
actually, we called Butt-Head, even&nbsp;
though that wasn't his name. Then there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:03.200
was a kid in the neighborhood about three&nbsp;
blocks away, his name was Bobby Beavis."

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:07.440
Judge says that the real-life Beavis&nbsp;
was nothing like the cartoon character:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:12.320
he just liked the name. The characters'&nbsp;
distinct voices came from real-life, too:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:16.080
Judge explained to The Daily Bruin&nbsp;
that Beavis' high-pitched cackle

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.280
"Came from this guy in my calculus&nbsp;
class that would sit in the front,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:24.080
and always bite his lip and turn back and go 'heh&nbsp;
heh.' That to me just turned into his laugh."

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.760
As for Butt-Head, Judge says that the&nbsp;
inspiration for him came from his childhood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.840
having once dealt with uncomfortable&nbsp;
dental problems. Judge revealed:

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.880
"I had braces in high school,&nbsp;
and they'd scrape your cheek,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:40.640
and you ended up talking like Butt-Head.That's&nbsp;
how I got the voice for Butt-Head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:42.880
just a guy talking with a mouth full of wires."

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.040
Not counting a couple of shorts&nbsp;
that aired on Liquid Television,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:53.840
the first episode of Beavis and Butt-Head debuted&nbsp;
on MTV on March 8, 1993, to immediate success.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:57.520
Network Vice President Abby&nbsp;
Terkuhle told the Los Angeles Times:

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:02.720
"They're perfect for MTV. They like and respond&nbsp;
to things that our audience finds humorous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:06.000
Maybe our audience can see a little bit&nbsp;
of themselves in Beavis and Butt-Head."

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.440
MTV knew it had a hit on its hands and made&nbsp;
plans to produce merchandise and feature the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:17.120
duo elsewhere in its programming, all before&nbsp;
its premiere. Unfortunately, plans took a turn:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:22.960
just a week after its premiere, MTV halted the&nbsp;
show's three daily airings. With just a handful of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:27.840
animated segments, the network had to rerun them&nbsp;
endlessly during premiere week because Judge and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:32.720
his animation team had fallen behind. Instead&nbsp;
of showing the same few episodes for weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:38.240
MTV pulled the show momentarily and brought it&nbsp;
back in May 1993 with plenty of new material.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:41.760
"Hey Butthead, how long till&nbsp;
something good's on TV?"

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:45.840
"Uh, two hours, dude."

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:51.920
Beavis and Butt-Head watch TV, attend high&nbsp;
school, and slack off at work in a sleepy little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:57.680
town called Highland. Its sandy terrain, constant&nbsp;
sun, and hot temperatures suggest that Highland is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:03.760
located somewhere in the American Southwest, such&nbsp;
as New Mexico or Texas. The ambiguous location of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:08.640
Highland was Mike Judge's intention: he said&nbsp;
in an interview at the Austin Film Festival,

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:12.400
"I grew up mostly in Albuquerque. And&nbsp;
I was in Dallas when I created it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.680
so I was thinking somewhere in between&nbsp;
those, like, just one of those,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:19.200
like a little town [...] like Lubbock,&nbsp;
or you know, Portales, New Mexico."

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:25.920
When the 1996 film Beavis and Butt-Head Do America&nbsp;
went into production, an animator slapped Texas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:31.200
license plates on some cars in background shots.&nbsp;
Judge was happy to go along with it, joking,

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:32.320
"So now it's Texas."

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:37.760
Half of any Beavis and Butt-Head episode&nbsp;
involves the titular characters wreaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:40.160
havoc on their school and around the neighborhood.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.680
The other half involves the characters&nbsp;
sitting on a filthy couch and offering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:49.120
savage critiques of music videos. Once in a while,&nbsp;
Beavis and Butt-Head would come across a band that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:54.080
"rocks" or "rules," and viewers would get a little&nbsp;
animation of the guys head-banging or dancing.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:05:01.120
Musical artists that received this exposure and&nbsp;
an on-air seal of approval often experienced major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:06.240
boosts in record sales. Before Beavis&nbsp;
and Butt-Head debuted in early 1993,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:12.880
the band White Zombie had sold just 75,000&nbsp;
copies of its 1992 album La Sexorcisto:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:18.480
Devil Music Vol. 1. After the cartoon featured&nbsp;
a White Zombie video, sales quadrupled, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:24.800
the album hit the Billboard top 40 chart. Babes&nbsp;
in Toyland's little-noticed 1992 LP Fontanelle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:30.080
started selling about 1,000 copies a week&nbsp;
after "Bruise Violet" hit Beavis and Butt-Head.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:35.120
For a rock band in the 90s, getting your video&nbsp;
on the show was a recipe for musical success.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:40.160
For every band that enjoyed the exposure&nbsp;
they got from Beavis and Butt-Head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.360
there was another that took offense&nbsp;
when the show made fun of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:47.760
In one episode, the boys briefly view an&nbsp;
Aerosmith clip, and come to the conclusion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:52.080
that the band is pretty cool — even if&nbsp;
they're old. According to Mike Judge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:57.440
Aerosmith pressured MTV into taking it out, and&nbsp;
they never watched another Aerosmith video again.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:02.080
On another occasion, MTV once told&nbsp;
Judge to stop making fun of the hair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.240
band Winger because the channel, quote,&nbsp;
"had a good relationship with them."

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:09.520
Even if an artist liked the show,&nbsp;
their music could still get nixed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:12.640
During the brief 2011 Beavis&nbsp;
and Butt-Head revival,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:18.480
Kanye West wanted the duo to watch and riff on&nbsp;
one of his videos. Judge told Rock News Desk:

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:22.640
"He wanted it on, and then somebody who owns,&nbsp;
like, six percent of the songwriting said no."

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:24.800
"Uh. Yeah."

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:27.520
"Makes sense to me, yeah."

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:32.160
Beavis and Butt-Head weren't exactly what most&nbsp;
parents would call good kids. They catcalled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.800
women, they were rude to adults, they listened&nbsp;
to loud and aggressive heavy metal music,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:41.280
and they loved playing with fire. Beavis in&nbsp;
particular was a pyromaniac, and in early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:45.760
episodes, he'd flick a lighter and shout so&nbsp;
often that it was basically a catchphrase.

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:50.720
In October 1993, about six months after&nbsp;
Beavis and Butt-Head premiered on MTV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:56.240
a five-year-old Ohio boy named Austin Messner&nbsp;
got a hold of a lighter and set his bed on fire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:01.680
The blaze quickly spread throughout his family's&nbsp;
mobile home. Messner and his mother, Darcy Burk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:05.680
escaped but the boy's two-year-old&nbsp;
sister unfortunately died in the fire.

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:11.600
Burk and local authorities placed the blame for&nbsp;
the tragedy squarely on Beavis and Butt-Head.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:15.840
She claimed to have recently caught her son&nbsp;
playing with matches and removed his bedroom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:20.960
door so she could keep an eye on him. Area Fire&nbsp;
Chief Harold Sigler told The New York Times:

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:24.640
"When you take a child in the formative&nbsp;
years, and you get these cartoon characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:28.160
saying it's fun to play with fire, this&nbsp;
is going to stick in that kid's mind."

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:33.200
MTV spokesperson Carole Robinson promised&nbsp;
the network would look into the issue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:38.480
and ultimately ended up removing all references&nbsp;
to fire from past and future episodes of the show.

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:44.080
In 2008, Messner, who was 20 years old at the&nbsp;
time, came forward with his side of the story:

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:48.400
"I literally NEVER saw the&nbsp;
cartoon. How could I? It was 1993,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:51.520
my Mom was a drug addict.&nbsp;
We couldn't afford cable!"

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:55.600
"It's stupid just to blame a TV show for&nbsp;
the problems that a family is having.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:59.600
I mean it's not real, anybody&nbsp;
knows, that's not real people."

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:03.760
Beavis and Butt-Head spawned&nbsp;
all kinds of tie-in merchandise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:09.440
including the 1993 Geffen Records album The Beavis&nbsp;
and Butt-Head Experience. It included a cover of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:16.400
Sonny and Cher's 1965 hit "I Got You, Babe," as&nbsp;
performed by Beavis, Butt-Head and the real Cher.

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:20.320
In the cover, Beavis and Butt-Head explain&nbsp;
how the legendary performer checks all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:26.400
boxes for what the duo wants in a woman. During a&nbsp;
spoken interlude in the song, Butt-Head asks Cher:

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:30.160
"Didn't you used to be married&nbsp;
to that Bono dude from U2?"

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:36.000
When she clarifies the situation,&nbsp;
explaining she was married to Sonny Bono,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:39.440
Butt-Head calls her ex-husband a&nbsp;
"wuss," and Cher agrees, saying:

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:40.800
"[...] Well kinda, yeah."

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:44.560
The exchange was improvised&nbsp;
in the studio in one take.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:47.360
Geffen executive Tony Berg&nbsp;
told the Los Angeles Times:

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:50.480
"She was not just a willing&nbsp;
participant, but also she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:53.920
got into the self-deprecation&nbsp;
of it and how funny it was."

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:59.040
Cher's ex-husband didn't find it funny,&nbsp;
though. Sonny Bono told the Los Angeles Times:

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:04.800
"I've got very thick skin so I would probably lose&nbsp;
about one second of sleep over the whole deal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:08.720
But in a way I'm sad to see Cher&nbsp;
reduce herself to that kind of humor."

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:13.760
Rightfully considered problematic today,&nbsp;
Beavis and Butt-Head debuted one of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:20.240
silliest and most memorable bits in 1994:&nbsp;
Beavis's alter ego, The Great Cornholio.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:24.720
Whenever he rapidly consumes massive&nbsp;
quantities of coffee or sugar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:29.520
Beavis pulls the neck of his T-shirt over his&nbsp;
head and declares himself "The Great Cornholio."

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:38.400
"You can take me, but you cannot take my bunghole,&nbsp;
for I have no bunghole. I am the Great Cornholio."

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:42.800
Using a quasi-Latin American accent,&nbsp;
Beavis typically causes mayhem as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:47.200
Great Cornholio while demanding toilet&nbsp;
paper from strangers. If anyone dares&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:52.240
interact with Beavis while he's in Cornholio&nbsp;
mode, he yells out his rhetorical catchphrase:

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:53.040
"Are you threatening me?"

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:58.960
The character seems like a baffling collection of&nbsp;
nonsense, but its origin comes from a true source.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:00.800
Mike Judge told Phoenix New Times:

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:04.240
"It just hit me like a ton of bricks&nbsp;
one night when I was about to go to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:08.080
sleep — Beavis should pull his tee-shirt&nbsp;
over his head and start babbling."

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:13.760
Judge doesn't remember where the name Cornholio&nbsp;
originated, but can pinpoint the inspiration for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:19.200
the voice — an irate customer from when he worked&nbsp;
as a movie theater ticket-taker. He explained:

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:23.280
"I had to tell people to wait outside. And&nbsp;
this one Middle Eastern guy came up and said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:28.320
'No! I will not wait outside!' [...] And I said I&nbsp;
was sorry but those were the rules, and he said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:31.840
'Are you threatening me?' He kept&nbsp;
saying it, and it stuck in my mind."

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:37.600
When Beavis and Butt-Head originally aired on&nbsp;
MTV in the mid-1990s, the channel was primarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:42.800
an outlet for music videos. The title characters,&nbsp;
who were music-loving metalheads, fit seamlessly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:48.240
into the format. Each animated episode was broken&nbsp;
up with segments of the duo watching pop and rock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:54.400
videos on MTV while offering commentary ranging&nbsp;
from unabashed praise to pointed putdowns.

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:58.560
Beavis and Butt-Head creator Mike Judge&nbsp;
also voiced both Beavis and Butt-Head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:03.760
and in order to record the video commentary,&nbsp;
he employed a particular but loose process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:06.400
He told Wild Cartoon Kingdom in 1994:

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:09.200
"I just go in and improvise a&nbsp;
lot of them. A lot of times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:12.240
one of the writers will come and just&nbsp;
hang out and kind of help out. But we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:15.520
just watch the video a bunch of times and&nbsp;
then I go into the booth and mess around."

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:19.920
Segment producer Susie Lynn would&nbsp;
edit Judge's bits and jokes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:25.040
merging them with clips from the music videos and&nbsp;
recycled animation from the show. Judge revealed:

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:28.800
"All the cutaways of B&amp;B during&nbsp;
the videos are all pulled from past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:31.120
shows. Once in a while we'll animate one."

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:37.360
Despite being each other's best and only friends,&nbsp;
Beavis and Butt-Head routinely cut each other down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:42.480
with crass, crude, and demeaning insults.&nbsp;
Most are full of words we can't say here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:46.320
but there's one that stuck out for its&nbsp;
timbre, creativity, and reference to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:51.680
something more profane: dill weed. Plenty&nbsp;
of kids and teenagers in school hallways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:56.080
imitated what they heard on Beavis and Butt-Head,&nbsp;
lobbing the same insults at each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.080 --> 00:12:02.160
Unlike the other phrases, however, "dill weed" was&nbsp;
actually created and coined by Mike Judge himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:06.880
It's an adaptation of an insult he'd used&nbsp;
and heard as a kid. Judge told The Awl:

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:08.800
"When I was in junior high in the '70s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:13.520
people would call each other d—weed. It was&nbsp;
a pretty common insult. I tried to do it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:17.440
'Beavis and Butt-Head' and standards and&nbsp;
practices at MTV wouldn't let me say it."

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:22.160
During a recording session, he&nbsp;
improvised the word "dillweed" instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:24.320
and it stuck. Judge elaborated:

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:28.720
"It kind of got a laugh, and it cleared with the&nbsp;
network censors, so I just kind of kept doing it."

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:35.040
After more than 200 episodes, the hit movie&nbsp;
Beavis and Butt-Head Do America, and millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:41.760
of teens imitating The Great Cornholio, Beavis and&nbsp;
Butt-Head ended its run on MTV in November 1997.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:47.520
The two-part finale featured a live Thanksgiving&nbsp;
special with MTV News personality Kurt Loder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:51.440
and a final animated installment&nbsp;
called Beavis and Butt-Head Are Dead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:56.400
Of course, Beavis and Butt-Head don't really die:&nbsp;
they just forget to go to school for three weeks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:59.280
When someone from Highland High&nbsp;
calls to talk with a parent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:02.960
Beavis claims that he and Butt-Head&nbsp;
are dead to get them off the phone.

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:07.360
So why did the show end? Well, it was&nbsp;
time for creator Mike Judge to walk away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:08.960
He told the Los Angeles Times:

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:12.880
"I actually wanted to stop a little&nbsp;
sooner. After the second season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:16.560
I thought, 'How are we gonna do this&nbsp;
anymore?' I was completely burnt out."

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:19.280
So by 1997, he wondered:

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:22.320
"Why not retire before it&nbsp;
gets too stale or whatever?"

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:24.560
"Hey Butthead, are we gonna die?"

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:28.960
"Uh, probably."

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:32.320
Judge immediately moved on to other projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.320 --> 00:13:36.480
such as the second season of his successful&nbsp;
animated Fox sitcom King of the Hill.

00:13:37.520 --> 00:13:42.640
Daria Morgendorffer, a cynical, smart,&nbsp;
exasperated Highland High School classmate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:47.520
of Beavis and Butt-Head, didn't appear in Mike&nbsp;
Judge's original Beavis and Butt-Head cartoon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:51.040
or any of the other early&nbsp;
shorts. According to HuffPost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:56.320
she was created after Judge sold the show to MTV&nbsp;
in order to flesh out the world of the series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.320 --> 00:14:00.480
Judge's inspiration for the character&nbsp;
was independent cartoonist Lynda Barry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:04.960
and her semi-autobiographical&nbsp;
comic strips. Judge told Vulture:

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:09.440
"I partly based the character Daria on how&nbsp;
I imagine Lynda Barry would have been in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:13.760
high school. I drew Daria dressed like Lynda&nbsp;
would dress when she went on 'Letterman.'"

00:14:14.560 --> 00:14:19.040
After Beavis and Butt-Head became a smash&nbsp;
hit, Judge and the rest of the writing staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:23.440
slowly eased Daria out of the show.&nbsp;
According to The New York Times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:29.120
viewers expressed their praise to MTV for the&nbsp;
minor character. MTV writer Glenn Eichler said:

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:33.200
"Basically, she was the smart person who hung out&nbsp;
with Beavis and Butt-Head to annoy her parents."

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:38.800
In 1995, MTV issued a directive to its&nbsp;
collective of writers and producers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:42.400
to make an animated show with a&nbsp;
young female viewership in mind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:46.400
Eichler suggested to executives that they&nbsp;
didn't need to create anything new — they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:53.280
could simply spin off Daria onto her own show.&nbsp;
Thus, Daria ran on MTV from 1997 to 2001.

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:59.840
In 2009, Mike Judge wrote and directed the&nbsp;
comedy Extract, with Jason Bateman starring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:04.400
as a flavor extract plant owner dealing with&nbsp;
a troubled marriage and problems at work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.400 --> 00:15:08.480
Earning middling reviews and only $10&nbsp;
million at the American box office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:14.000
Extract was a rare misfire for Judge — but it did&nbsp;
lead to a brief revival of Beavis and Butt-Head.

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:14.720
"Are we dead?"

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:17.360
"I guess not."

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:20.560
"Oh, yeah yeah."

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:24.160
Judge told Collider that during&nbsp;
a marketing meeting for Extract,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:28.560
someone came up with the idea of a promotional&nbsp;
clip featuring Beavis and Butt-head watching a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:33.120
scene from the movie and commenting about it&nbsp;
on their famous couch. Now throw in another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:38.800
relative flop from Judge — the short-lived&nbsp;
2009 ABC cartoon The Goode Family — and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:43.920
stars aligned for a full-scale revival&nbsp;
of Beavis and Butt-Head on MTV in 2011.

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:46.800
MTV didn't show many music videos anymore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:52.080
so the guys had to provide snarky commentary&nbsp;
on MTV reality shows like Jersey Shore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:55.840
Unfortunately, the new Beavis and&nbsp;
Butt-Head lasted just one season.

00:15:55.840 --> 00:15:58.960
It wasn't all bad news for Mike&nbsp;
Judge in the world of TV, though:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.960 --> 00:16:03.520
he would go on to create the hit&nbsp;
HBO series Silicon Valley in 2014.

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:08.320
In the summer of 2019, Beavis&nbsp;
and Butt-Head creator Mike Judge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:13.360
revealed to /Film that another movie&nbsp;
may be on the horizon. Judge said:

00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:16.320
"I think it would have to be something&nbsp;
that makes it relevant today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:18.560
I think I might've figured out a way to do that."

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:23.440
Indeed, it would seem that he figured it&nbsp;
out. Paramount, the parent company of MTV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.440 --> 00:16:28.560
announced in 2022 that before the year was out,&nbsp;
a new movie featuring the stupid twosome would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:34.160
hit its streaming service, Paramount+. According&nbsp;
to TheWrap, Beavis and Butt-Head Do the Universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:39.520
the spiritual successor to 1996's&nbsp;
big-screen Beavis and Butt-Head Do America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.520 --> 00:16:46.080
was described by Paramount as "perhaps the&nbsp;
dumbest space movie ever made." Beginning in 1998,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:51.280
a judge with a penchant for creative sentencing&nbsp;
orders Beavis and Butt-Head to attend space camp.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.280 --> 00:16:56.800
From there, the pair accidentally makes their way&nbsp;
onto the Space Shuttle, then deep into the cosmos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.800 --> 00:17:02.160
before eventually falling through a black hole and&nbsp;
returning to Earth in the 2020s. Of course, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:07.680
movie will also serve as a launchpad to an entire&nbsp;
new season of the Beavis and Butt-Head TV series.

